Your task is:
Task:
  ID: 42
  Title: [HIGH] dispatch: CVE-2026-1234
  Description: idna accepts Punycode labels that do not produce any non-ASCII output
  EpicId: 9

This task is part of epic #9: CVE
If you need help, or a sibling task's agent is working on a similar problem, ask that agent.

This is a CVE remediation task. It is a targeted dependency fix, not a feature.
Do not design it spec-first, and do not write a failing test first — neither
matches the shape of this work.

Start from the advisory. The description carries the summary; the task's URL
points at the alert. Identify the vulnerable package, the affected range, and
the exact versions the advisory lists as fixed. Only a version in that fixed set
counts: a higher version number is not automatically patched, because fixes are
backported to maintenance branches and a newer minor released earlier can still
be vulnerable.

1. Find where this repo pins that package — a manifest, a lockfile, or a
   transitive dependency of something it pins.
2. Apply the smallest change that lands on a version in the fixed set.
3. If no fixed version exists, or reaching one needs source changes beyond the
   pin, stop and tell the user what you found. Do not invent a workaround.
4. Run the repo's verify command and confirm it passes.
5. Add a test only where the fix changed our own code rather than a pinned
   version. The rule above is against test-first design of a version bump, not
   against testing a patch you wrote yourself.

Knowledge base: the `/learnings` skill manages it — check it when something is unclear, and record what you find.

When your work is done — finishing implementation, or (for an epic-decomposition task) creating work packages for its subtasks, and not before — use the dispatch:wrap-up skill to commit any remaining changes and finalise the task.
